  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "How many pairs \\((m,n)\\) of integers satisfy the equation \\(m+n=mn?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "more than \\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Because \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) must be integers, it helps to turn the equation into a product with very few integer factor pairs. Rearranging and adding \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm+n=mn\n&\\iff mn-m-n+1=1\\\\\n&\\iff (m-1)(n-1)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTwo integers have product \\(1\\) only when both are \\(1\\) or both are \\(-1\\). Thus\n\\[\n(m-1,n-1)=(1,1)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad(-1,-1),\n\\]\nwhich gives\n\\[\n(m,n)=(2,2)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad(0,0).\n\\]\nBoth pairs satisfy the original equation. Every solution must come from one of these two factor pairs, and each factor pair determines exactly one ordered pair \\((m,n)\\). Therefore there are exactly \\(2\\) pairs, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
